for (i = 0; i < NUM_DAIOTYP; i++) {
    if (((i == MIC) && !cap.dedicated_mic) || ((i == RCA) && !cap.dedicated_rca))
        continue;
    da_desc.type = (atc->model != CTSB073X) ? i : ((i == SPDIFIO) ? SPDIFI1 : i);
Reason:
The loop iterates over all DAIOTYP enum entries, including SPDIF1 (SPDIFI1), which is not valid on hw20k2 hardware because no corresponding DAIO definition exists. The skip condition only handles MIC and RCA, so SPDIF1 is not skipped, leading to an invalid resource allocation that causes a kernel crash.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (((i == MIC) && !cap.dedicated_mic) ||
    ((i == RCA) && !cap.dedicated_rca) ||
    i == SPDIFI1)
    continue;
if (atc->model == CTSB073X && i == SPDIFIO)
    da_desc.type = SPDIFI1;
else
    da_desc.type = i;
Reason:
The added condition `i == SPDIFI1` skips the invalid SPDIF1 type for all models except CTSB073X, where it is used as a replacement for SPDIFIO. The assignment logic is restructured to set `da_desc.type` to SPDIFI1 only when the model is CTSB073X and the current index is SPDIFIO, otherwise using the index directly. This ensures that SPDIF1 is never used unless explicitly mapped to SPDIFIO on the supported model, preventing the crash.

